WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.870
Disney's animated classic Aladdin is one of
the studio's most treasured movies, but after

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.980
many years of rewatching it, fans have unearthed
some surprising plot holes, problems and mistakes

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.459
in the original animation.

00:00:11.459 --> 00:00:15.980
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm explaining how the live-action

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.150
remake of Aladdin fixes many of those issues
from the 1992 movie.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.440
Some spoilers ahead so take care if you haven't
seen the film yet.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.070
And don't forget my Aladdin giveaway is also
running, so be sure to subscribe and leave

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:28.930
a comment for your chance to win.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:33.270
Let's kick off with the popular fan theory
that the Genie failed to grant Aladdin all

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.770
of his three wishes in the original movie.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:36.770
"Wish fulfillment?"

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.239
"Three wishes, to be exact.

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:40.950
And ixnay on the wishing for more wishes."

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.489
It's the second wish where the Genie saves
Aladdin from drowning which is the most problematic.

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:50.260
In both the animation and the new film, Aladdin
is tied up and has passed out so even though

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.079
his hand brushes against the lamp, he can't
ask for a wish from the Genie.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.280
"You can't cheat on this one.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.090
I can't help unless you make a wish.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.460
You have to say, "Genie, I want you to save
my life."

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.460
Got it?"

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.600
Many fans think that because Aladdin never
explicitly asked for his second wish

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.820
"Now, is that an official wish?

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.189
Say the magic words."

00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:10.170
That technically the Genie never gave that
second wish to Aladdin.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.549
The new movie has a neat little fix for this
issue by introducing the idea of "grey areas"

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:16.440
when it comes to genies granting wishes.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.979
"Hey, can you make me a prince?"

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:22.790
"There is a lot of grey area in 'make me a
prince'."

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.330
So, when Aladdin is on the verge of drowning
in the new movie, the Genie creates a back-dated

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.470
contract and moves Aladdin's hand to sign
it.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:34.700
The document which is dated the day before
Aladdin was pushed into the sea declares that

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.520
for his second wish he wants "to be saved
from certain doom."

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.170
This "grey area" that the Genie uses is a
sneaky way to resolve how many wishes were

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:48.180
granted to Aladdin and it's also used again
later in the movie when the Genie grants Jafar

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.790
his three wishes.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.850
By the way, the filmmakers are clearly aware
that the audience may be checking on how many

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.469
wishes are granted in the new movie because
just before he grants Aladdin his first wish

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.890
to become a prince, the Genie says this is
"an official wish for those of us who are

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.539
counting."

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:08.420
In the original "A Whole New World" song,
Aladdin and Jasmine fly all the way from Agrabah,

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:12.880
past the pyramids in Egypt, through Greece
and finally end up in China all in just one

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:13.880
night.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.730
Although it's supposed to be a fantastical
sequence in the animation, it's a pretty tall

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.500
feat over such a short time even for a magic
carpet.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.130
As explained in the official Art and Making
of Aladdin book, the live-action movie aims

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.981
to give this scene a greater sense of realism,
so instead of an international flight, this

00:02:30.981 --> 00:02:36.260
time the magic carpet takes Jasmine and Aladdin
on a tour of the area and coastline surrounding

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.390
Agrabah.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:41.190
Changing the locations in the magic carpet
ride and finishing back in Agrabah rather

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.720
than China also gives an opportunity for the
film to further highlight Jasmine's love for

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.760
her people and country.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.380
And speaking of Princess Jasmine, there are
some problematic moments in the original animation

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.090
that the filmmakers were clearly keen to avoid
in the new movie.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:03:01.670
Jasmine was only 15 years old in the original
film, being a few days away from turning 16.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:06.340
And so the scene where she seduces Jafar to
distract him from Aladdin's arrival feels

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.380
rather icky.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.030
For the remake, Disney obviously didn't want
to recreate a scene of a 15- or 16-year old

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.740
Jasmine in chains and a skin-baring outfit
who has to seduce a much older man, and is

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.040
then trapped in an hourglass.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.290
That part of the old movie is scrapped in
the new version and updated by giving Jasmine

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:28.740
much more agency and her own song "Speechless",
the name of which, by the way, is a neat call-back

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.480
to the moment in the animated movie where
the young princess was being forced to marry

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.420
Jafar:
"You will wed Jafar."

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.770
"You're speechless, I see.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.790
A fine quality in a wife."

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:42.590
"I will never marry you!"

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:47.120
In both stories, Jafar's final wish is his
undoing as he ends up trapped as a genie in

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.120
a magic lamp.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.640
"You wanted to be a genie...
you got it."

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.640
"What?"

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:53.640
"And everything that goes with it."

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:54.640
"Phenomenal cosmic powers..."

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:55.640
"You're the genie.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:56.640
I don't want..."

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.060
"Itty bitty living space."

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.010
The problem with how it happens in the original
film is that because Jafar is already in command

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:06.550
of the Genie, he really should have realised
that becoming a genie would make him a captive.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:11.410
The original film basically relies on Jafar
acting rather stupidly at this point.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.320
The new movie fixes this issue in two steps.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.560
First of all, Jafar specifically asks to become
"the most powerful being in the universe",

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.810
more powerful than the Genie himself.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:26.660
Notice that Jafar doesn't ask to become a
genie, presumably because he realises that

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.410
would involve him being enslaved.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:32.800
Secondly, the Genie uses some of the latitude
he has when it comes to interpreting wishes

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.290
to purposely give Jafar a bad outcome.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:41.440
Just before granting Jafar's wish, the Genie
craftily mutters, "a lot of grey area in that",

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.010
then turns him into a powerful giant red genie,
which, of course, dooms Jafar to live inside

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.570
a new magic lamp.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:52.600
The big change made to the Genie's behaviour
in the new movie is that the "grey area" he

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.100
has when granting wishes means he can be a
lot more manipulative.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.910
For example, when Aladdin made his first wish,
the Genie went out of his way to warn him

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.410
that he needed to be careful with his words.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.350
"Be specific in your words.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.480
The deal is in the detail."

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:12.040
However, the Genie deliberately didn't offer
Jafar the same advice and instead decided

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:16.380
to interpret the villain's final wish as negatively
as possible.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.880
Something that fans of the original animation
had wondered about for years was whether the

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:24.261
Peddler who introduced the story of Aladdin
and the magic lamp was meant to be the Genie

00:05:24.261 --> 00:05:28.540
in human form, especially as Robin Williams
voiced both characters.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:31.262
"Ah, salaam, and good evening to you, worthy
friend.

00:05:31.262 --> 00:05:32.720
Perhaps you would like to hear the tale?

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.660
It begins on a dark night..."

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.560
In fact, as the filmmakers acknowledged in
a Blu-ray commentary for the film, they had

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.020
always planned for the connection between
the Peddler and the Genie to be completely

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.950
obvious by having the Peddler reappear at
the very end, just after Jasmine and Aladdin's

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:54.140
final kiss, as the original workprint for
the movie also shows.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.400
"Ah happy end to the tale!

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.720
Are you sure you don't want to buy this lamp?

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.380
Like I told you, things are never quite what
they seem, are they?

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:06.380
Hahahahaha.."

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:10.400
The good news is that in the 2019 movie, it's
very clear that the Genie and the narrator

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.210
at the beginning of the film are one and the
same, which fixes the lost connection from

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.650
the 1990s animation.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:21.310
When Prince Ali parades into Agrabah and stomps
into the palace on his elephant, it's a truly

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.590
spectacular and fun moment in the original
movie.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.710
However, it seems rather strange that everyone
appears to simply take the new Prince Ali

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:33.230
at his word about where he's from and whether
he's even a real prince.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:34.680
"Prince Ali Ababwa.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:35.760
Of course.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.450
I'm delighted to meet you."

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:41.490
To fix this and make things more realistic
in the live-action movie, it's made clear

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:46.710
that both Jasmine and Jafar have never heard
of Ababwa and they both investigate further.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:52.150
Jasmine confronts Prince Ali, telling him
that she can't find Ababwa on any of her maps.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:56.620
Aladdin does manage to continue his ruse with
a little help from the Genie who adds Ababwa

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.520
onto one of Jasmine's maps, although Jafar
figures out quite quickly that Ababwa is a

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.960
made-up place.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.491
In the original animation, just before the
Cave of Wonders allows Aladdin to enter, it

00:07:07.491 --> 00:07:09.340
instructs him:
"Proceed.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.300
Touch nothing but the lamp."

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.970
Although those instructions seem pretty straightforward,
in other words, "don't touch anything except

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:21.490
the lamp", some fans have quibbled that there's
a few occasions where Aladdin and Abu do just

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:22.710
that with no penalty.

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:27.410
For example, they both have contact with the
magic carpet and Aladdin also clearly puts

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.710
his hands on some of the rocks at one point.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:33.810
The live-action movie fixes this issue by
modifying the cave's rules.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:38.840
This time Jafar tells Aladdin to "take no
other treasure" and "to take nothing but the

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:39.840
lamp".

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.420
It's a subtle change, but the difference is
that in the animation the rule was basically

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.710
"don't touch" and in the live-action it's
"don't take".

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.360
That small rule change clarifies that it's
OK to touch or step on anything as long as

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.420
you don't try to take it.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:57.230
This is doubly-important in the new movie,
because the cave is littered with treasure

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:01.669
everywhere so much so that it's impossible
for Aladdin to navigate his way through without

00:08:01.669 --> 00:08:03.610
touching any of it.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.949
The depiction of the characters and culture
in the original Aladdin is something that

00:08:06.949 --> 00:08:10.900
Disney's been criticised for as it draws on
numerous negative stereotypes.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:16.650
When the film was released in 1992, the lyrics
to the opening song "Arabian Nights" originally

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:20.690
included the line "Where they cut off your
ear if they don't like your face".

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:24.550
To address some of the criticism around that
song, for the home entertainment release Disney

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:29.820
changed the line to "Where it's flat and immense
and the heat is intense", but did retain the

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.120
next line "It's barbaric, but hey, it's home".

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.810
Many still objected to use of the word "barbaric"
and so for the remake, Disney rewrote much

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:41.010
of the song to avoid such controversy this
time around.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:44.670
Likewise in the new movie, when Jasmine can't
pay for the food she gives to a couple of

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:49.640
poor children, the merchant doesn't threaten
to chop off her hand like he did in the animation.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.180
"Do you know what the penalty is for stealing?"

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:56.960
There is a little nod to the original moment
though in how he grabs hold of her arm, but

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.640
that's as far as it goes.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.310
The "Prince Ali" lyrics have also been corrected
and updated.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:06.540
The original line "Sunday Salaam" has been
changed to "Friday Salaam" to reflect correctly

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.150
that Friday is the Muslim holy day.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:13.089
And the line in "Prince Ali" that "he's got
slaves, he's got servants and flunkies" has

00:09:13.089 --> 00:09:17.440
had the 'slaves' reference removed and been
updated to "he's got ten thousand servants

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.520
and flunkies".

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.550
Jasmine and Aladdin's clothing in the new
film has also been updated to be more practical,

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:25.480
realistic and less revealing.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.860
It's unlikely that a princess would have walked
around in that top all of the time, so now

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:34.660
Jasmine gets some beautiful new outfits but
which keep her midriff covered up.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.950
The costume designers added a nod to Jasmine's
bare midriff in the animation by adding a

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:42.920
flesh-coloured piece of fabric in the tummy
area of Jasmine's dress.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:47.640
Similarly, in the animated movie, Aladdin
is bare-chested when he isn't Prince Ali,

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.960
but Disney covered him up with a shirt so
they wouldn't have a topless Aladdin in the

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.200
live-action version.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.970
And the three harem girls in the "One Jump
Ahead" song have also been replaced this time

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.900
around with a group of modestly-dressed schoolgirls.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:03.870
Now, did you spot any plot holes or mistakes
in the new movie?

